¬}\pard \s12 \li1463 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql NSResponder\
\fs80\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s31 \li4231 \fi-2719 \ri1007 \ql \tx4233 \f0 \b \fs24 Inherits From:\tab \f1 \b0 \fs28  NSObject\
\fs20\
\pard \s21 \li4233 \fi-2721 \ri1007 \ql \tx4233 \fs28 \f0 \b \fs24 Conforms To:\tab \f1 \b0 \fs28 NSCoding\
\fi0 NSObject\'80(NSObject)\
\fs20\
\s22 \li4233 \fi-2721 \fs28 \f0 \b \fs24 Declared In:\tab \f1 \b0 \fs28 AppKit/NSResponder.h\
\fs20\
\fs20\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s10 \li1209 \fi-1007 \ri1007 \ql Class Description\
\fs14\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 NSResponder is an abstract class that forms the basis of event and command processing in the Application Kit. The core classes\'d0NSApplication, NSWindow, and NSView\'d0inherit from NSResponder, as must any class that handles events. The responder model is built around three components: event messages, action messages, and the responder chain. An \i event message\i0  is a message corresponding directly to an input event, and includes as its sole argument an NSEvent object describing the event; a mouse down or keypress, for example. An \i action message\i0  is a higher-level message indicating a command to be performed, which includes as an argument the object requesting the action. Some examples of action messages are the standard \b cut:\b0 , \b copy:\b0 , and \b paste:\b0 .\
\fs14\
\fs28 The \i responder chain\i0  is a series of responder objects to which an event or action message is applied. When a given responder object doesn\'27t handle a particular message, the message is passed to its successor in the chain. This allows responder objects to delegate responsibility to other, typically higher-level objects. The responder chain is constructed automatically as described below, but you can insert custom objects into it using the \b setNextResponder:\b0  method and examine it with \b nextResponder\b0 .\
\fs14\
\fs28 An application can contain any number of responder chains, but only one is active at any given time. It begins with the \i first responder\i0  in some NSWindow and proceeds to the NSWindow itself. The first responder is typically the \'aaselected\'ba NSView within the NSWindow, and its next responder is its containing NSView (also called its superview), and so on up to the NSWindow itself. You can safely inject other responders between NSViews, but you can\'27t add responders past the NSWindow. Nearly all event messages apply to a single window\'27s responder chain.\
\fs14\
\fs28 For action messages, a more elaborate responder chain is used, constructed from the individual responder chains of two NSWindows and the application object itself. The NSWindows are the \i key window\i0 , whose responder chain gets first crack at action messages, and the \i main window\i0 , which follows. The main window is sometimes identical to the key window; the two are typically distinguished when an auxiliary window or panel related to a primary window\'d0such as a Find Panel\'d0is opened. In this case the primary window, which was the key window, becomes the main window, and the Find Panel becomes key. The two windows and the NSApplication object also give their delegates a chance to handle action messages as though they were responders, even though a delegate isn\'27t formally in the responder chain (a \b nextResponder\b0  message to one of these objects doesn\'27t return the delegate). Given all these components, then, the full responder chain comprises these objects:\
\fs14\
\pard \s6 \li1810 \fi-298 \ri1007 \ql \tx1810 \tx3376 \tx3753 \fs28 \'b7\tab The key window\'27s first responder and successors, including objects added with \b setNextResponder:\
\b0 \'b7\tab The key window itself\
\'b7\tab The key window\'27s delegate (which need not inherit from NSResponder)\
\'b7\tab The main window\'27s first responder and successors, including objects added with \b setNextResponder:\
\b0 \'b7\tab The main window itself\
\'b7\tab The main window\'27s delegate (which need not inherit from NSResponder)\
\'b7\tab The application object, NSApp\
\'b7\tab The application object\'27s delegate (which need not inherit from NSResponder)\
\fs40\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s59 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql Selecting the First Responder\
\fs8\
\fs6\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 The first responder is typically chosen by the user, with the mouse or keyboard. The mechanism by which one object loses its first responder status and another gains it is public though, and you can programmatically change the first responder if necessary. The method that changes the first responder is NSWindow\'27s \b makeFirstResponder:\b0 . An NSWindow\'27s first responder is initially itself, though you can set which object will be first responder when the NSWindow is first placed on-screen using the \b setInitialFirstResponder:\b0  method.\
\fs14\
\fs28 \b makeFirstResponder:\b0  always asks the current first responder if its ready to resign its status, using \b resignFirstResponder\b0 . If the current first responder returns NO when sent this message, \b makeFirstResponder:\b0  fails and likewise returns NO. If the current first responder returns YES then the new one is sent a \b becomeFirstResponder\b0  message to inform it that it can be the first responder. This object can return NO to reject the assignment, in which case the NSWindow itself becomes the first responder.\
\fs14\
\fs28 When an NSWindow that\'27s the key window receives a mouse-down event, it automatically tries to make first responder the NSView under the event. It does so by asking the NSView whether it wants to become first responder, using the \b acceptsFirstResponder\b0  method defined by this class, with the mouse-down event as the argument. This method normally returns NO; responder subclasses that need to be first responder must override it to return YES. This method is also used when the user changes the first responder using the keyboard.\
\fs14\
\fs28 Normally a mouse-down event in a non-key window simply brings the window forward and makes it key, and isn\'27t sent to the NSView over which it occurs. The NSView can claim an initial mouse-down, however, by implementing \b acceptsFirstMouse:\b0  to return YES. The argument is the mouse-down event, which the NSView can examine to determine whether it wants to receive the mouse event and potentially become first responder.\
\fs14\
\fs28 An additional consideration for responders that manage selections is of course to set the selection. An NSView that handles mouse events should set this itself. However, objects can also define methods for setting their selection that automatically make the receiver first responder as well. NSTextField\'27s \b selectText:\b0 , for example, does something quite like this.\
\fs14\
\fs26\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s59 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql Event and Action Messages in the Responder Chain\
\fs8\
\fs6\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 The main purpose of the responder chain is to route events and action messages to an appropriate target. Event and action methods are dispatched in different ways, by different methods. Nearly all events enter an application from the Window Server, and are handled automatically by NSApplication\'27s \b sendEvent:\b0  method. Action messages are instigated by objects, who use NSApplication\'27s \b sendAction:to:from:\b0  method to route them to their proper destinations.\
\fs14\
\fs28 NSApplication\'27s \b sendEvent:\b0  analyzes the event and handles some things specially\'d0key equivalents, for example. Most events, however, it passes to the appropriate window for dispatch up its responder chain using NSWindow\'27s \b sendEvent:\b0  method. NSResponder\'27s default implementations of all event methods simply pass the message to the next responder, so if no object in the responder chain does anything with the event it\'27s simply lost. As mentioned before, an NSView\'27s next responder is nearly always its superview, so if, for example, the NSView that receives a \b mouseDown:\b0  message doesn\'27t handle it, its superview gets a chance, and so on up to the NSWindow. If no object is found to handle the event, the last responder in the chain invokes \b noResponderFor:\b0 , which for a key-down event simply beeps. Event-handling objects (subclasses of NSWindow and NSView) can override this method to perform additional steps as needed.\
\fs14\
\fs28 Event messages form a well-known set, so NSResponder provides implementations for all of them. Action messages, however, are defined by custom classes and can\'27t be predicted. For this reason they\'27re dispatched in different manner from events. To instigate an action message, an object invokes NSApplication\'27s \b sendAction:to:from:\b0 . The first argument is the selector for the action method to invoke. The second is the intended recipient of the message, often called the \i target\i0 . The final argument is usually the object invoking \b sendAction:to:from:\b0 , thus indicating which object instigated the action message. If the intended target isn\'27t \b nil\b0 , the action is simply sent directly to that object; this is called a \i targeted action message\i0 . In the case of an \i untargeted action message\i0 , where the target is \b nil\b0 , \b sendAction:to:from:\b0  searches the full responder chain for an object that implements the action method specified. If it finds one, it sends the message to that object with the instigator of the action message as the sole argument. The receiver of the action message can then use the argument directly as input or query it for additional information. You can find the recipient of an untargeted action message without actually sending the message using \b targetForAction:\b0 .\
\fs14\
\fs28 A more general mechanism, which applies to the shorter form of the responder chain, is provided by NSResponder\'27s \b tryToPerform:with:\b0 . This method checks the receiver to see if it responds to the selector provided, if so invoking the message. If not, it sends \b tryToPerform:with:\b0  to its next responder. NSWindow and NSApplication override this method to include their delegates, but they don\'27t link individual responder chains in the way that NSApplication\'27s \b sendAction:to:from:\b0  does. Similar to \b tryToPerform:with:\b0  is \b doCommandBySelector:\b0 , which takes a method selector and tries to find a responder that implements it. If none is found, the method beeps.\
\fs14\
\pard \s69 \li2721 \fi-1209 \ri1007 \ql \tx2721 \fs28 \f0 \b \fs24 Warning:\tab \f1 \b0 \fs28 NSResponder declares a number of action messages, but doesn\'27t actually implement them. You should never send an action message directly to a responder object of an unknown class. Always use NSApplication\'27s \b sendAction:to:from:\b0 , NSResponder\'27s \b tryToPerform:with:\b0  or \b doCommandBySelector:\b0 , or check that the target responds using the NSObject method \b respondsToSelector:\b0 .\
\fs14\
\fs26\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s59 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql Implementing Event and Action Methods\
\fs8\
\fs6\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 Implementing event methods is fairly straightforward. If your subclass handles a particular event, it overrides the method\'d0\b keyDown:\b0 , for example\'d0usurping the implementation of its superclass. If your subclass needs to handle particular events some of the time\'d0only some typed characters, perhaps\'d0then it must override the event method to handle the cases it\'27s interested in and to invoke \b super\b0 \'27s implementation otherwise. This allows a superclass to catch the cases it\'27s interested in, and ultimately allows the event to continue on its way along the responder chain if it isn\'27t handled. \'aaKey Events,\'ba below, describes how to handle keyboard events in your application. See the NSView class specification for information on handling mouse events.\
\fs14\
\fs28 Action methods don\'27t have default implementations, so responder subclasses shouldn\'27t blindly forward action messages to \b super\b0 . Passing of action messages is predicated merely on whether an object responds to the method, unlike with the passing of event messages. Of course, if you know that a superclass does in fact implement the method, you can pass it on up from your subclass.\
\fs14\
\fs26\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s59 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql Key Events\
\fs8\
\fs6\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 Processing keyboard input is by far the most complex part of event handling. The Application Kit goes to great lengths to ease this process for you, and in fact handling the key events that get to your custom objects is fairly straightforward. However, a lot happens to those events on their way from the hardware to the responder chain. The sections below attempt to explain how events are handled through the operating system and the Application Kit, so that you can understand what your objects receive and don\'27t receive.\
\fs14\
\fs26\
\f0 \b \fs24 \pard \s60 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql The Path of a Key Event\
\fs8\
\fs6\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 Physical keyboard events must pass through the operating system before becoming NSEvent objects in the Application Kit. Depending on the operating system, some of these \'aaraw\'ba events might be trapped before that ever happens. Reserved key combinations are often trapped in this way. Key events that arrive at the Application Kit are processed by NSApplication\'27s \b sendEvent:\b0  method as indicated before. The application object filters out key equivalents (also known as \'aaCommand key events\'ba) and sends them out as described below under \'aaKey Equivalents and Mnemonics.\'ba All other key events are passed to the key window\'27s \b sendEvent:\b0  method.\
\fs14\
\fs28 The key window first checks the event to see if the Control key is pressed. If it is, the window treats the event as a forced control event, which is blocked from the responder chain and is processed immediately as a potential mnemonic or keyboard interface control event. If this doesn\'27t apply, the event is passed to the window\'27s first responder in a \b keyDown:\b0  message, which is how your custom responders receive uninterpreted key events. \'aaKeyboard Input\'ba describes how you can handle these events.\
\fs14\
\fs28 If no view object in the key window accepts the key event, NSWindow\'27s \b keyDown:\b0  attempts to handle the key event itself. It tries to interpret the key event as each of the following, in order, beeping if it fails to match any of them to let the user know that the typing couldn\'27t be processed:\
\fs14\
\pard \s6 \li1810 \fi-298 \ri1007 \ql \tx1810 \tx3376 \tx3753 \fs28 \'b7\tab A mnemonic matching the character(s) typed, not requiring the Alternate key to be pressed\
\'b7\tab A key equivalent, not requiring the Command (or Control) key to be pressed\
\'b7\tab A keyboard interface control event\
\fs40\
\f0 \b \fs24 \pard \s60 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql Key Equivalents and Mnemonics\
\fs8\
\fs6\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 A key equivalent is a character bound to some view in a window, which causes that view to perform a specified action when the user types that character, usually while pressing the Command key (the Control key on Microsoft Windows). A mnemonic works similarly, using the Alternate key as its cue to action. If both modifier keys are pressed, the key event is interpreted only as a mnemonic. A key equivalent or mnemonic must be a character that can be typed with no modifier keys, or with Shift only. Each is sent down the view hierarchy of a window instead of up the responder chain, but at different times.\
\fs14\
\fs28 Key equivalents are dispatched by the NSApplication object\'27s \b sendEvent:\b0  method. On the Mach operating system, this results in a \b performKeyEquivalent:\b0  message being sent to every NSWindow in the application until one of them returns YES. On the Microsoft Windows operating system, it results in a \b performKeyEquivalent:\b0  message being sent to the menu of the key window, and of the main window if the key window\'27s menu doesn\'27t handle it. This difference in handling means that, among other things, NSWindow subclasses shouldn\'27t override \b performKeyEquivalent:\b0 . Also, objects other than menu items shouldn\'27t be assigned key equivalents; they should instead be assigned mnemonics. Key equivalents sent to a window on Mach are passed down the view hierarchy through NSView\'27s abstract implementation of \b performKeyEquivalent:\b0 , which forwards the message to each of its subviews until one responds YES, returning NO if none does.\
\fs14\
\fs28 Mnemonics, on the other hand, are dispatched by the key window. If the user presses the Control key as well as the mnemonic\'27s key combination, NSWindow\'27s \b sendEvent:\b0  immediately treats that event as a mnemonic to be performed, without sending the event up the responder chain. If the user doesn\'27t press the Control key, the event passes through the window\'27s responder chain, possibly being handled by a responder, before arriving as a \b keyDown:\b0  message to the window. In either case, a mnemonic for a top-level menu on Microsoft Windows is sent back to the operating system, and eventually results in the Application Kit invoking a menu item\'27s action. Any other mnemonic is handled by sending a \b performMnemonic:\b0  message down the window\'27s view hierarchy, in the same manner as for a \b performKeyEquivalent:\b0  message.\
\fs14\
\pard \s46 \li2310 \fi-798 \ri1007 \ql \tx2308 \fs28 \f0 \b \fs24 Note:\tab \f1 \b0 \fs28 \b performKeyEquivalent:\b0  takes an NSEvent as its argument, while \b performMnemonic:\b0  takes an NSString containing the uninterpreted characters of the key event. You should extract the characters for a key equivalent using NSEvent\'27s \b charactersIgnoringModifiers\b0  method.\
\fs40\
\f0 \b \fs24 \pard \s60 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql Keyboard Interface Control\
\fs8\
\fs6\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 Mnemonics are actually part of a more general means of controlling the user interface via the keyboard. An NSWindow treats certain key events specially, as commands to move control to a different interface object, to simulate a mouse click on it, and so on. In brief, pressing Tab moves control to the next object, whether a button, a text field, or some other kind of control object. Shift-Tab moves control to the previous one. Pressing Space simulates a mouse click for many kinds of control objects, causing a push button to click, a radio button to toggle its state, and so on. In selection lists, pressing Space selects or deselects the highlighted item; the user can also press Alternate or Shift to extend the selection, not affecting other selected items. Some interface controls also accept arrow-key input.\
\fs14\
\fs28 Each window can be assigned a default button, which is triggered by the Return or Enter key. Also, in modal windows or panels the user can press the Escape key to dismiss the window or panel. If interface control moves to another button, the default button temporarily loses this ability as the user\'27s focus shifts to the button where control resides. However, if control then moves to a different kind of interface object, the default button resumes its normal ability.\
\fs14\
\fs28 The interface objects that are connected together within a window make up the window\'27s \i key view loop\i0 . You normally set up the key view loop using Interface Builder, establishing connections to each interface object\'27s \b nextKeyView\b0  outlet. You can also set the object that\'27s initially selected when a window is first opened by setting the window\'27s \b initialFirstResponder \b0 outlet in Interface Builder. NSView and NSWindow also define a number of methods for manipulating the key view loop programmatically; see their class specifications for more information.\
\fs14\
\fs26\
\f0 \b \fs24 \pard \s60 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql Keyboard Input\
\fs8\
\fs6\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 A normal key event eventually makes its way to the responder chain as a \b keyDown:\b0  message, which the receiver can handle in any way it sees fit. A text object typically interprets the message as a request to insert text, while a drawing object might only be interested in a few keys, such as Delete and the arrow keys to delete and move selected items. The receiver of a \b keyDown:\b0  message can extract the event\'27s character\'27s directly using NSEvent\'27s \b characters\b0  or \b charactersIgnoringModifiers\b0  methods, or it can pass the key event to the Application Kit\'27s input manager for interpretation according to the user\'27s key bindings. Input management allows key events to be interpreted as text not directly available on the keyboard, such as Kanji and some accented characters, and as commands that affect the content of the responder object handling the event. See the NSInputManager and NSTextInput class and protocol specifications for more information on input management and key binding.\
\fs14\
\fs28 To invoke the input manager, simply invoke NSResponder\'27s \b interpretKeyEvents:\b0  message in your implementation of \b keyDown:\b0 . This method sends an NSArray of events to the input manager, which interprets the events as text or commands and responds by sending \b insertText:\b0  or \b doCommandBySelector:\b0  to your responder object. The section below, \'aaStandard Action Methods for Selecting and Editing,\'ba describes the messages that your object might get sent.\
\fs14\
\fs26\
\f0 \b \fs24 \pard \s60 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql Standard Action Methods for Selecting and Editing\
\fs8\
\fs6\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 NSResponder declares prototypes for a number of standard action methods, nearly all related to manipulating selections and editing text. These methods are typically invoked through \b doCommandBySelector:\b0  as a result of interpretation by the input manager. They fall into the following general groups:\
\fs14\
\pard \s6 \li1810 \fi-298 \ri1007 \ql \tx1810 \tx3376 \tx3753 \fs28 \'b7\tab Selection movement and expansion\
\'b7\tab Text insertion\
\'b7\tab General deletion of elements\
\'b7\tab Modifying selected text\
\'b7\tab Scrolling a document\
\fs14\
\fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 In most cases the intent of the action method is clear from its name. The individual method descriptions in this specification also provide detailed information about what such a method should normally do. However, a few general concepts apply to many of these methods, and are explained here.\
\fs14\
\fs28 \b Selection Direction\b0 . Some methods refer to spatial directions; left, right, up, down. These are meant to be taken literally, especially in text. To accommodate writing systems with directionality different from Latin script, the terms\i  forward\i0 , \i beginning\i0 , \i backward\i0 , and \i end\i0  are used.\
\fs14\
\fs28 \b Selection and insertion point\b0 . Methods that refer to moving, deleting, or inserting imply that some elements in the responder are selected, or that there\'27s a zero-length selection at some location (the insertion point). These two things must always be treated consistently. For example, the \b insertText:\b0  method is defined as replacing the selection with the text provided. \b moveForwardAndModifySelection:\b0  extends or contracts a selection, even if the selection is merely an insertion point. When a selection is modified for the first time, it must always be extended. So a \b moveForward...\b0  message extends the selection from its end, while a \b moveBackward...\b0  message extends it from its beginning.\
\fs14\
\fs28 \b Marks\b0 . A number of action methods for editing text imitate the Emacs concepts of \i point\i0  (the insertion point), and \i mark\i0  (an anchor for larger operations normally handled by selections in graphical interfaces). \b setMark:\b0  establishes the mark at the current selection, which then remains in effect until the mark is changed again. \b selectToMark:\b0  extends the selection to include the mark and all characters between the selection and the mark.\
\fs14\
\fs28 \b The kill buffer\b0 . Also like Emacs, deletion methods affecting lines, paragraphs, and the mark implicitly place the deleted text into a buffer, separate from the pasteboard, from which you can later retrieve it. Methods such as \b deleteToBeginningOfLine:\b0  add text to this buffer, and \b yank:\b0  replaces the selection with the item in the kill buffer.\
\fs14\
\fs26\
\f0 \b \fs28 \pard \s59 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql Other Uses\
\fs8\
\fs6\
\f1 \b0 \fs28 \pard \s3 \li1512 \fi0 \ri1007 \ql \tx3603 \tx3981 \tx4359 The responder chain is utilized by two other mechanisms in the Application Kit. In enabling and disabling a menu item, the application object consults the full responder chain for an object that implements the menu item\'27s action method, as described in the NSMenuActionResponder protocol specification. Similarly, the Services facility passes \b validRequestorForSendType:returnType:\b0  messages along the full responder chain to check for objects that are eligible for services offered by other applications. The Services validation process is described fully in \'aaServices\'ba in \i OPENSTEP Programming Topics\i0 .\
\fs14\
